WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.780
An agent can't be asked
for a second factor,

2
00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.750
so everything it may do
is decided in advance.

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.188
Start with the credential.

4
00:00:07.188 --> 00:00:12.306
In Agent ID it lives on the blueprint and
is shared by every agent created from it,

5
00:00:12.306 --> 00:00:15.175
so the blueprint is the
credential boundary.

6
00:00:15.175 --> 00:00:20.076
The order is settled: a managed identity
federated to the blueprint where the agent

7
00:00:20.076 --> 00:00:24.621
runs on Azure, workload identity federation
from its own platform elsewhere,

8
00:00:24.621 --> 00:00:27.675
a certificate in a vault
only where neither applies,

9
00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:30.587
and a client secret only
for development and test.

10
00:00:30.587 --> 00:00:31.762
Then privilege.

11
00:00:31.762 --> 00:00:34.924
Microsoft blocks agents
from Global Administrator,

12
00:00:34.924 --> 00:00:38.374
but still allows Exchange
and SharePoint Administrator.

13
00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:40.579
That's a floor, not least privilege,

14
00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.537
so the program's rule is that no AI
identity holds a standing privileged role.

15
00:00:45.537 --> 00:00:48.762
Last, an agent's reach is
bounded by its tokens,

16
00:00:48.762 --> 00:00:51.638
its approved tools, and data protection.

17
00:00:51.638 --> 00:00:54.930
In GCC, Purview's agent
controls aren't available,

18
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.030
so scoped permissions
are the data boundary.
